CONDITIONAL EXECUTION - In order to allow simple decisions to be taken by the  
run command system, a conditional test instruction is included which takes the 
result of the last page comparison performed and will branch on that result.   
                                                                         quence, as this will trigger the initial delay. This sequence will be 
ignored if no external delay has been set, and therefore it is normal to use   
it at the start of every command file.                                         
                            
When 2 pages are compared (function 13 of MAIN MENU) a flag is set if the pages
are different in any way (this includes attributes such as colour, height). The
headers are not compared, so the date and string are ignored. Page layout is   
signi                                                         
IMPORTANT NOTE - the VIEWTEXT system is a highly "parallel" system, and will   
respond to keyboard inputs immediately when the modem is "live". In order to   
protect the incoming pages, time deficant. The flag can be tested, and if the test fails all commands up to  
the next (\) will be skipped. The test command (0) will skip if the flag was   
set, and (1) will skip if the flag was clear. The flag state is not changed.   
If the () sequencelays MUST be used between a page request    
and the next run command file character. The delay should be long enough to    
receive a full page. For example, the sequence:-                               
                                                 is used, no test is made, but the flag is complemented. This
allows the flag to be used without a comparison ie. (0)()  sets the flag.      
                                                                               
13\ (1) 12\ C:LIST\ O\ R\\ (\)                                
*799! [20] (P) *800! [20] (!)(!)   will fetch page 799, wait for 20 seconds,   
switch screens, fetch page 800, wait for 20 seconds and then exit to the main  
menu (assuming that ! is used as the HASH character).        (!)(!)            would test 2 displayed pages. 
If the pages were different, the right page would be saved in the drive C file 
LIST.PGE overwriting any existing file, then unconditional exit to MAIN MENU.  
