TERMINATION OF COMMAND FILES - when the command file has been prepared, an end 
of file marker must be added to it. This marker is a CTRL-Z character (ASCII   
value 26 decimal, 1A hex). This is a safety marker to prevent VIEWTEXT retory no 1 with a number and your    
password and id, and then repeat the pre-dialling sequence described in section
4.5 of the User Guide. A logged-on disc should be in drive B.                  
                                                        ading 
past the end of the command file in the command buffer in the event of a format
error. This is only needed for editors which do not automatically add CTRL-Z,  
and can be added using the file transfer utility PIP.COM as shown:             
                            
This command file will dial the number, read 2 pages (799 and 800), save 799   
on the disc (drive B) if different, save 800, and then load them to the file   
CAROUSEL and display them with a 10 second delay. The keyboard will b                                                                          
A>PIP B:filename.ext = B:filename.ext,EOF:           for CP/M systems.         
                                                                               
If the VIEWTEXT editor is used to create or edit run command files the file    
extension .PRC must be used. This ensures that the file is saved without the   
date and string header, which would otherwise confuse the command interpreter. 
Any lines which are conditionalrepetitive tasks can be placed 
in files, and with a combination of commonly used command files and a small    
amount of keyboard input, complex operations can be performed easily.          
                                                              (using the (0) or (1) codes) must be terminated
with the (\) sequence, as this marks the end of a conditional section.         
                                                                               
